{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"FINAL ENTRY (2026-08-07, session close)\n\nWORK COMPLETED SINCE THE HANDOFF ENTRY\n\n* 7084 CLOSED. R1/R3/R5 merged (Gerrit 1507/1508/1510). R1 measured 28x-557x on the real 71k-commit store; the 155-file ticket went 1165.54s -> 2.09s, and the after-column no longer grows with event count, so the unbounded growth is gone. Acceptance criteria were REMEDIATED rather than force-ticked: original AC1 described R2 (hoist the ledger out of the lock), which was declined because at ~1s there is no reason to accept its race; original AC2 asked for a worktree field the operator's existing-machinery scope for R5 excludes.\n* 9fb3 CLOSED, merged bcd77a8eaf. Dropping the unused inspect-ai dependency also removed BOTH [tool.uv] conflicts entries and the click override, each proved unnecessary by re-running the resolver rather than assumed: uv sync --locked --extra dev --extra bedrock now INSTALLS (the pair the fork forbade), the lock reaches click 8.4.2 unaided, pip-audit clean. Lock diff was pure removal, 46 packages dropped, zero version changes to anything retained. The [eval] extra was removed entirely.\n* e6e9 CLOSED, merged a55b93a0f5. Option A implemented with real per-mutation success plumbing, not an approximation of the pass exit code. Deliberately all-or-nothing per update_issue call, on the reasoning that under-reporting makes the baseline LAG (next pass re-pushes) while over-reporting makes it LEAD, which never self-corrects. RED proof by neutering the fix to pre-fix behaviour: 7 failed / 9 passed, with the held-firm and soft-fail cells staying GREEN through the neutering — proving they pin behaviour the fix must not change. Live-path direction preservation added for all five mirrored fields WITH a runtime reachability assertion, closing the gap where those cells had only ever driven a function with no production caller.\n* 24f7 CLOSED with a passing verifier, no force.\n* 10c3 FILED (P1): a write whose lock acquire times out is PERMANENTLY LOST. Measured 6/6 concurrent comments lost at 60s. First acceptance criterion is to RE-MEASURE against post-R1 main before building anything — at ~1s holds the timeout may now be unreachable, which would rescope it.\n* 3b93 FILED: entering the review-bot app lifespan in a test inherits a 1200s shutdown drain. Prevention only (bound SHUTDOWN_DRAIN_SECONDS globally in tests + an autouse fixture making the stubbed path default); an AST lint was considered and explicitly rejected as poor-signal. Linked discovered_from 89d5.\n* 291e FILED: canary_bridge's bare 'import alert_dedup' resolves only when scripts/ is on sys.path. SECOND regression from 0767c38ebb within hours.\n* 89d5 (py3.11 cell) enriched with four occurrences and, importantly, a CORRECTION: the 99%-hang mode is NOT always runner-side. An A/B against clean origin/main @1afdbbd0be proved one instance was a real test defect — 'with TestClient(app)' entering the app lifespan and inheriting a 1200s drain. The two modes are distinguishable from the log (hang = progress percentage, orphaned children, no summary; crash = names the test and the gw worker) and need OPPOSITE responses.\n\nSTILL OPEN AT SESSION END\n* 9f63 in_progress, Gerrit 1512 at patch set 7 (LLM-Review +1 PASS). Its agent is doing a single recheck; the Verified -1 is the known py3.11 worker-crash, not its change.\n* 10c3, 3b93, 291e, 535e (idea), 858c, 2876, 81d5, 556a, 8fbd, 514d — all with decisions or evidence recorded.\n\nTWO PROCESS LESSONS I LEARNED THE HARD WAY THIS SESSION\n* Do NOT act on a Gerrit change an agent owns. gerrit-verify.yaml sets cancel-in-progress keyed on the change id, so a stray recheck KILLS the in-flight run and restarts a ~70-minute clock. I did this on 1505 while its agent was managing it. The right move is SendMessage to the owner, which worked well on 1512.\n* Never print an unconditional success echo after a store write. Two writes in this session's tail were silently lost to lock timeouts while my echo claimed success; only a follow-up read caught it. Verify by re-reading, always.\n\nR5 CONFIRMED WORKING IN PRODUCTION: a lost write surfaced as 'flock: could not acquire lock after 60s (holder: host=... pid=17905 held=117s pid_state=live)'. The old message said only '60s'. Note held=117s is well above R1's ~1s target — useful evidence for 10c3."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786142247288705001,"uuid":"d148e099-e9fe-4e7a-8c32-f272c31696cc"}